WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:35.850
Consider the car.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:43.420
Cruise, commute, sit in traffic, take a road
trip, make out in the back seat, break down,

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:57.280
get pulled over, push the pedal to the metal,
race, chase, crash.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.670
The car has a ubiquitous place in cinema.

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:02.550
But why is cinema obsessed with the automobile?

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:08.740
One of the most fetishized objects in film,
the car is represented more often, and more

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:12.370
diversely than any other form of transportation.

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:22.170
But to call the car transportation, is to
misunderstand its role in film entirely.

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:27.290
The car movie, and the large car chase action
set pieces are probably what leap immediately

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:31.790
to mind when thinking of cars in movies, and
you could make a whole video essay about those

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:33.260
things alone.

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:37.740
But the space the car has carved out in the
movies is much larger than that.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:44.720
The car is a costume.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:49.750
A character’s car can tell you a lot about
a them before they even speak.

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:55.060
In Nightcrawler, Lou’s car is the main way
we’re shown that he’s gaining success.

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:59.420
In Boyhood, the car the father drives tips
us off the the fact that he’s the “fun

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:05.030
irresponsible dad” and when he trades it
in later in the film, it’s an indication

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:10.200
of personal change.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:19.430
A 
car can be an extension of a character, in

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:23.370
The Darjeeling Limited the brother’s attempts
to retrieve their father’s broken down car

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:30.330
from the garage on the way to his funeral
illustrates their dysfunction in the midst

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:40.440
of their grief.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:50.360
In The Big Lebowski, … the destruction of
the Dude’s car parallels the out-of-control

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:53.570
spiral of the story.

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:58.190
In Taxi Driver, Travis’ Bickle’s Taxi
is a high horse for him to sit upon.

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:08.160
It is what separates him from what he calls
the scum of the street.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:10.049
The car is a location.

00:03:10.049 --> 00:03:14.190
The interior is a dramatic stage.

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:19.070
The characters can all face the audience and
the scenes that unfold inside a car are as

00:03:19.070 --> 00:04:01.570
varied as the humans that occupy them.

00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:06.030
This forced intimacy is instant fodder for
what cinema loves to examine.

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:23.870
Though equally interesting to the prying eyes
of the film camera are the inescapable silences,

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:32.230
and the arguments.

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:45.270
Cars trap us in a box with other people who
can’t leave, at least without hurting themselves.

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:58.970
There’s the tension of being forced into
the back seat with a few unsavory characters,

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:01.780
or the joy of being crammed together with
some buddies.

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:04.610
A car can separate a character from their
surroundings.

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:06.700
It’s a portable space we own.

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:10.640
It’s a representation of our individualism,
of our personal freedom.

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:14.890
In the car you get to go where you want to
go, your hands are on the wheel.

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:17.270
You feel you control your destiny.

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:32.650
You get to put your music on.

00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:37.860
That space provides a, perhaps false, sense
of privacy shelter from the outside world.

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:51.010
Criminals feel free to talk business.You 
might try to hide in one from outside danger.

00:05:51.010 --> 00:06:00.880
But that space can be invaded, revealing how
fragile a shell of protection it really is.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:08.670
And the freedom the car gives us can leave
us vulnerable.

00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:12.620
The Car as a location includes more than just
the interior though.

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:16.900
The archipelago of cars in a parking lot are
personal islands for people to gather around

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:18.520
for conversation.

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:23.270
Leaning at someone’s car window puts you
on the line between stranger and friend, waiting

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:27.450
to be welcomed in, or evaluating whether you’d
even want to enter.

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:32.030
A character might remove someone from their
car as an attempt to remove that person from

00:06:32.030 --> 00:06:35.280
their life.

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:43.620
Or drive away to achieve the same effect.

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:47.790
The car can symbolizes youth grasping for
maturity and trying to enter adulthood.

00:06:47.790 --> 00:06:53.690
In Rushmore, Max’s constant invasion of
adult’s cars is an image of his attempts

00:06:53.690 --> 00:06:55.400
to be a part of their world.

00:06:55.400 --> 00:07:00.320
In Lady Bird, Lady Bird’s need for others
to drive her around is an image of her dependence

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:05.550
and then eventual gaining of independence.

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:25.560
The car 

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:26.850
is violent.

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:30.830
The trauma of a accident has been used endlessly
as a dramatic tool.

00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:36.139
A visual shorthand, it’s a favorite of the
flashback.

00:07:36.139 --> 00:07:41.020
Vandalizing a car isn’t an attack on an
object, it’s an attack on the person who

00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:42.590
drives that car.

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:47.610
The car itself can become a way to act out.

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:52.880
In the Godfather, there is a motif of violence
in association with cars.

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:56.640
Perhaps representing how changing times are
presenting challenges for the Mafia, a car

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:00.160
is a dangerous, vulnerable place to be for
a mobster.

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:04.360
Maybe the car harnesses some more primal instinct
for the thrill of the chase.

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:10.110
It’s a mundane and common place part of
our lives that can suddenly become a vehicle

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:22.560
of thrill, a weapon, and gadget, or a detective’s
tool for investigation.

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:27.400
Enticing to the camera is the movement, the
endless motion of the landscapes found outside

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:28.780
the windows.

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:34.080
There can be a dramatic inevitability to this
movement, a feeling of something that can’t

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:35.080
be undone.

00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:40.630
Locke, a movie set entirely in a car during
a single trip, uses the finality of each mile

00:08:40.630 --> 00:08:53.730
driven to build tension.

00:08:53.730 --> 00:08:58.149
But that movement can also come to a screeching
halt, leaving people stuck, unable to get

00:08:58.149 --> 00:09:27.720
where they want to go, creating some of the
conflict and obstacles every character needs.

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:29.740
The obsession runs deep.

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:33.190
Even in our fantasies we cling to the image
of the car.

00:09:33.190 --> 00:09:35.400
We use it to traverse through time.

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:39.690
Even when society has been utterly destroyed,
the car remains.

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:44.521
We consistently humanize the car and imbue
upon it a personality and self, making it

00:09:44.521 --> 00:09:59.050
one of the few pieces of technology we seem
to consistently anthropomorphize in our media.

00:09:59.050 --> 00:10:03.000
Scenes in cars have been shot in a variety
of ways throughout film history.

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:07.320
But there emerges what I see as two primary
approaches.

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:11.880
In the first, and most common, the camera
assumes an omniscient presence around the

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:12.880
vehicle.

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:17.740
Impossibly floating in and around the car
to provided the best compositions.

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:25.440
The second approach, rising to prominence
through the French New Wave, places the camera

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:34.130
in the car, the viewer just another passenger.

00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:41.770
But filmmakers have gone to great lengths
to film the car, it presents a challenge,

00:10:41.770 --> 00:10:44.920
but one that filmmakers are obviously willing
to overcome.

00:10:44.920 --> 00:10:50.580
I think the diversity of things that a car
can be or represent, makes it a perfect tool

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:56.770
for filmmakers.Certainly, Hollywood’s location
in a city where cars are a necessity has impacted

00:10:56.770 --> 00:10:58.820
the presence of cars in the movies.

00:10:58.820 --> 00:11:03.370
LA writers write cars into films because cars
are a part of their lives.

00:11:03.370 --> 00:11:08.291
The world’s empire of film had been located
in a city with public transportation, maybe

00:11:08.291 --> 00:11:09.920
things would be different.

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:14.710
Cinema is the technological sibling of the
car, born of the same era, neither knows of

00:11:14.710 --> 00:11:17.730
a time without the other.

00:11:17.730 --> 00:11:21.180
How will cinema be effected by the way the
car changes?

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:24.800
Will the arrival of self-driving cars be like
the cellphone?

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:29.370
An obstacle that writers will have to learn
to write for, something that challenges the

00:11:29.370 --> 00:11:32.800
conventions of how transportation is displayed.

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:37.740
Whether or not it continues to be, the car
has been a vital piece of cinematic storytelling.

00:11:37.740 --> 00:12:04.069
In 
a film a car is never just a car.

